# Cursor

This file only routes you. The contract is `AGENTS.md` in the repository root;
nothing here replaces it.

1. Before the first tool call, read `AGENTS.md` in full. Its rules are binding —
   above all: no authorship attribution in commits or pull requests, never push
   to `main` or push a tag, and the gate before calling anything done.
2. The evidence and completion requirements are in `AGENTS.md`,
   "Working method" and "Verification".
3. `CLAUDE.md`, if it is in your context, is written for Claude Code: its
   plugins, `.claude/settings.json` and tool names do not apply to you.
4. Skills: `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (symlinks to `.claude/skills/`, the
   same files). Open the one covering the task before acting; the map is in
   `AGENTS.md`, "Skills and documentation tools".
5. Path-scoped rules arrive through the other `.mdc` files here —
   `rust-source.mdc`, `shell-and-configs.mdc`, `docs-sources.mdc`,
   `ci-and-release.mdc`, `website.mdc`. Each points to the file whose text you
   follow.

## MCP

Context7 requires the API key. Use a user-level server that sends it from the
environment, e.g. `"headers": {"Context7-API-Key": "${env:CONTEXT7_API_KEY}"}`
in `~/.cursor/mcp.json`; the Cursor Context7 plugin authenticates with OAuth,
which the CLI cannot complete. Add `astro-docs`
(`https://mcp.docs.astro.build/mcp`) for website work. Usage rules:
`AGENTS.md`, "Skills and documentation tools".

## Headless runs

Started by skill `cursor-delegate` (`CURSOR_DELEGATE=1`), you run without
anyone to approve a prompt. `.cursor/hooks/delegate-guard.sh` denies git
mutations, GitHub writes, publishing and `rm -r`. On a denial or a decision
you cannot take from the brief, `AGENTS.md` or the code, stop and make the last
line of your answer `QUESTION: <question with options>`. Never retry a denied
command in another form.

## Authorship

Keep `attributeCommitsToAgent` and `attributePRsToAgent` off in your Cursor
config. If a trailer or footer still appears in text you prepare, remove it.
The `.githooks/commit-msg` hook backs this up; never bypass it.
